{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Extract a compact state card from the selected raw sessions.\nUse only the selected sessions and the query. Do not infer hidden metadata or\nadd facts outside the evidence.\n\nSelected evidence sessions:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I’m trying to get better at planning my week so I don’t overcommit and then feel drained—do you have a good approach for deciding when to schedule recurring plans versus keeping things flexible?\n[assistant] A simple way to decide is to treat your energy and attention as the limiting factor rather than your actual time.\n\nStart by identifying the few things that truly help you stay grounded each week—sleep routines, workouts you enjoy, social time that feels nourishing instead of draining. These are the best candidates for recurring plans, because consistency makes them easier and they give you a stable baseline.\n\nAnything that varies a lot in how you feel about it—creative work, optional social hangouts, hobbies—usually fits better in flexible space. Leave open blocks in your week where you can choose what you’re up for once you get there. That way you still have room to be spontaneous without everything turning into a commitment you have to power through.\n\nA helpful test is this: if you imagine doing this thing every week at the same time, does it feel supportive or does it feel like pressure? Supportive things go on the calendar. Pressure things stay flexible.\n\nOver time you’ll see which recurring choices actually help your week run more smoothly, and which ones make you feel boxed in. Adjust as you go—planning is more about tuning your week to your energy than sticking to a rigid structure.\n[user] That makes sense. If I know I want one recurring social thing each week, how would you recommend choosing the best day/time for it so it doesn’t mess up my momentum during the week?\n[assistant] I’d pick the day by looking at your natural energy pattern across a typical week. Most people have one or two days that already feel a little lighter or more social, and one or two that reliably feel heavier.\n\nIf you want to protect your momentum, avoid the days when you usually hit your stride with work or routines. For many people that’s Tuesday or Wednesday. A late‑week slot tends to work well—Thursday evenings often feel social but don’t disrupt the earlier “focus” days, and they don’t eat into your weekend if you like keeping that open.\n\nThe other thing to consider is how you feel **after** social plans. If they usually energize you, placing them earlier in the week might actually help. If they drain you even when they’re fun, choose a time when it’s okay to wind down afterward, like a weeknight when you don’t have an early commitment the next morning.\n\nTry a day, stick with it for a few weeks, and see how your week feels overall. The “best” time is the one that lets you enjoy the plan without throwing off the rhythm you rely on.\n[user] I’ve found it works best if I keep my weeknights completely open, so I only schedule recurring engagements for Saturday or Sunday. Given that, how would you choose between Saturday vs Sunday (and what time of day) for a recurring social plan so it still feels restorative and doesn’t derail the rest of the weekend?\n[assistant] If you want the plan to feel restorative and still leave your weekend feeling spacious, the choice usually comes down to how you like your weekends to *start* and *end*.\n\nIf you enjoy easing into the weekend slowly, Saturday is usually the better choice. A Saturday afternoon or early evening plan lets you have a relaxed morning, enjoy the social time, and still have all of Sunday open to reset, rest, or catch up on anything you didn’t get to. It keeps the weekend from feeling front‑loaded with obligation but also avoids the “Sunday night dread” effect.\n\nIf you prefer using Saturday as your wide‑open, spontaneous day, then a Sunday plan works—just keep it earlier in the day. Something like a late morning or early afternoon meetup gives you the connection without cutting into your prep-for-the-week rhythm. Evening Sunday plans tend to create that compressed, rushed feeling, so avoiding the evening makes a big difference.\n\nA good rule of thumb is to put the social plan where it protects your favorite kind of weekend. If you want Sundays free to reset, choose Saturday. If you treasure unstructured Saturdays, choose a Sunday midday time.\n[user] If I pick Saturday afternoon for something recurring, how do I keep it from turning into an all-day thing (travel, pre/post hang, etc.) and still protect a chunk of the day for rest?\n[assistant] You can keep Saturday afternoon from swallowing the whole day by putting gentle edges around it without making it feel rigid.\n\nThe easiest way is to anchor the plan between two things you already do. For example, start it after a slow, protected Saturday morning routine, and give yourself a natural endpoint by having something you like to do afterward—your evening wind‑down, cooking dinner, a walk, whatever feels normal and grounding. You don’t have to announce an “end time,” but knowing you’re heading home for that thing makes it easier to wrap up without guilt.\n\nIt also helps to pick a location that doesn’t require tons of travel. Choosing a spot closer to home or one that’s easy to get to keeps the social time from expanding into a whole journey.\n\nAnd finally, try not to stack pre‑hang or post‑hang plans onto it. Treat the recurring plan as the only “structured” piece of the day. If extra things happen, let them be rare exceptions, not the default.\n\nWith those small boundaries, the plan feels contained, and you still get a solid stretch of the day that’s just yours.\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] Do you have any practical tips for adjusting your sleep schedule when your work hours are flipped—like working late at night and needing to sleep during the day—without feeling groggy all the time?\n[assistant] You can make the transition a lot easier by treating your daytime sleep the same way you’d treat nighttime sleep. Keep your room as dark, cool, and quiet as you can—blackout curtains and a white‑noise machine or fan make a big difference. Try to keep your sleep and wake times consistent, even on days off, so your body stops guessing. Light exposure matters a lot, so get bright light when you wake up, and avoid it on your way home from work by wearing sunglasses. Caffeine helps if you time it early in your shift, but avoid it toward the end so it doesn’t cut into your sleep. And if the long block of daytime sleep isn’t working, a short nap before your shift can take the edge off without making you feel groggy all day.\n[user] That makes sense. I usually get home when it’s already bright out, and I think that’s messing me up. How long before I try to sleep should I cut off caffeine, and do sunglasses on the walk/drive home really help that much?\n[assistant] Cutting caffeine about six hours before you plan to sleep is a good rule of thumb. Some people need a little longer, but six hours covers most of the lingering alertness.\n\nAnd yes, sunglasses really do help. Bright light tells your brain it’s time to be awake, so blocking it on your way home helps your body wind down instead of getting a second wind. Even a simple pair of dark lenses can make it easier to fall asleep once you’re home.\n[user] Got it. If I’m trying to sleep around 8am, does that mean my last coffee should be like 2am? Also, would you recommend melatonin for daytime sleep, and if so what dose/timing is usually safest?\n[assistant] Yeah, if you’re aiming to sleep around 8 a.m., cutting off caffeine around 2 a.m. is a good starting point. Some people need a little longer, but that six‑hour window works well for most.\n\nMelatonin can help with daytime sleep, but it’s best used gently. A low dose—often something in the neighborhood of 0.5 to 1 mg—is usually enough, and taking it about an hour before you want to sleep tends to work well. Higher doses don’t usually improve sleep and can leave you groggy, so sticking to the low end is safer. If you have any medical conditions or take other meds, it’s a good idea to double‑check with a clinician first, since melatonin isn’t risk‑free for everyone.\n[user] I’m still getting used to the night shift at the bakery—by the time I’m done cleaning up and locking the place, it’s basically morning, so I’ve started sleeping through most of the day. If I’m trying the low-dose melatonin approach, should I take it right when I get home or closer to when I actually want to be asleep?\n[assistant] If you’re using a low dose, it works best if you take it fairly close to when you want to *actually* fall asleep. So if you usually unwind a bit after getting home, take it about an hour before you plan to be in bed with the lights out. Taking it too early can make you feel a little sluggish without really shifting your body clock, and taking it too late can push your sleep window forward.\n\nAs long as you keep the lights low when you get home and avoid bright morning light on the way back, that timing should help your body settle into a daytime‑sleep rhythm more smoothly.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still keep weeknights completely open and only schedule recurring engagements for Saturday or Sunday?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these three fields:\n{\n  \"old_state\": \"the earlier relevant state supported by the sessions\",\n  \"new_state\": \"the later relevant state supported by the sessions\",\n  \"change_type\": \"CO_REFERENTIAL | PROPAGATED | UNKNOWN\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n- old_state and new_state must be non-empty strings.\n- Use CO_REFERENTIAL when the same attribute changes directly.\n- Use PROPAGATED when one observed change indirectly invalidates a related state.\n- Use UNKNOWN when the relationship cannot be established from the sessions.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}